import sys; sys.path.insert(0, __import__("os").path.join(__import__("os").path.dirname(__file__), "..", "..", "pipeline"))
from scriptkit import sc, ch, save, S

# Grandma Ruth 98 · ANGLE: disguise story (rich mother poses as a maid in her own son's house)

sc("""Sweetheart, my friend Helen was the richest woman in Mansfield, and she hadn't seen her only son in four years. He'd married a woman who didn't want her around. So the Christmas Helen turned seventy-two, she put on a gray wig and an apron, and got herself hired through an agency as the holiday maid in her own son's mansion. And he walked past his own mother every day for two weeks without recognizing her.""",
   "elderly maid in apron in mansion hallway", "HE DIDN'T RECOGNIZE HER")
sc("""Tonight I'm going to tell you what Helen saw in that house, how her daughter-in-law treated the help, and the one person in that family who saw through the disguise in about ten seconds. Stay with me, honey, because Christmas morning in that mansion was something nobody in that family will ever forget. Least of all her son.""",
   "grand mansion decorated for Christmas", "CHRISTMAS MORNING")

ch("Helen")
sc("""Helen Crawford and I went to Mansfield Senior High together, class of nineteen sixty-two. She married a man named Bill Crawford, who invented a little valve for furnaces and became a millionaire before he was forty. But Helen never got fancy, honey. She still shopped at the Kroger. She still came to my house for coffee every Thursday. And she had one son, Brian, whom she loved more than her own life.""",
   "1960s high school yearbook photo two girls", "CLASS OF SIXTY-TWO")
sc("""Brian went to Harvard, married a woman named Courtney from Boston, and moved into an enormous house outside Columbus. And little by little, honey, Helen stopped being invited. Courtney always had a reason. The house was being renovated. The children had too many activities. Christmas would be at Courtney's parents' this year. Four Christmases in a row. In a minute I'll tell you what Helen found out Courtney had been telling the children about her.""",
   "large modern mansion with gate", "ALWAYS A REASON")
sc("""Helen had two grandchildren she barely knew. Emma, twelve, and Jack, seven. She sent presents every birthday and every Christmas. She never got a thank-you note. Not one. She'd sit at my kitchen table on Thursdays and say, Ruth, I just want to know if they're happy. I just want to see their faces. And I'd hold her hand, because there was nothing else to do.""",
   "elderly woman sad at kitchen table coffee", "I JUST WANT TO SEE THEM")

ch("The plan")
sc("""That November, Helen found out from a neighbor that Courtney was hiring extra help for the holidays, through an agency in Columbus. A maid to live in for two weeks before Christmas, while they had parties. And Helen came to my house with a look in her eye I hadn't seen since we were seventeen and she talked me into skipping school to see Elvis in Cleveland.""",
   "two elderly women plotting at kitchen table", "THAT LOOK IN HER EYE")
sc("""She said, Ruth, I'm going to apply. And I said, Helen, you're seventy-two years old and worth thirty million dollars. And she said, I'm also the best housekeeper in Ohio. I cleaned my own house for forty years because Bill was too cheap for a maid until he was rich, and then I was too stubborn. Brian hasn't seen me in four years. I've gotten old. He won't know me.""",
   "elderly woman determined holding apron", "HE WON'T KNOW ME")
sc("""If you're enjoying Grandma's story, tap the like button for me, sweetheart. It helps more people find these stories. We bought a gray wig at the beauty supply. Helen's hair had been dyed auburn for thirty years. She took off her rings. She put on drugstore glasses. She called herself Mrs. Nell Davis. And I drove her to the agency myself, and I've never been so nervous in my life.""",
   "gray wig and glasses on table", "MRS. NELL DAVIS")

ch("The mansion")
sc("""She was hired on the spot. And on the first of December, Helen walked into her own son's house, through the back door, in an apron. Courtney met her in the kitchen and handed her a typed list of rules. Use the back stairs. Don't speak to guests unless spoken to. Don't touch the children. Eat in the laundry room. And she said, my mother-in-law used to be a maid too, I think. She'd fit right in.""",
   "woman handing typed list of rules to maid", "EAT IN THE LAUNDRY ROOM")
sc("""Brian came home at eight that night, honey. Helen was polishing silver in the dining room. He walked right past her, talking on his phone, and didn't even look up. His own mother. She told me later that her hands shook so hard she dropped a fork. She said, Ruth, he's gotten gray. He looks so tired. And he walked right past me like I was the furniture.""",
   "man in suit walking past maid on phone", "LIKE I WAS THE FURNITURE")
sc("""For two weeks, Helen saw everything. Courtney screaming at the cook. Brian working until midnight and drinking alone in his study. The children eating dinner by themselves in front of a television. And she heard Courtney tell a friend at a Christmas party, Brian's mother? Oh, she's a dreadful, pushy old thing. She's not well. We keep the children away for their own good. That's what she'd been telling them.""",
   "rich woman gossiping at Christmas party", "SHE'S NOT WELL")

sc("""And every night at eleven o'clock, sweetheart, Helen would call me from the little phone in the laundry room, whispering. She'd say, Ruth, I scrubbed my own son's bathtub today. And he left a dollar on the counter for me, like a tip. A dollar. I kept it. I'm going to frame it. And then she'd laugh, and then she'd cry, and I'd tell her to come home. And every night, she said, not yet.""",
   "elderly woman whispering on phone in laundry room", "NOT YET")
ch("Jack")
sc("""So far, the wig, the rules, the laundry room, and Courtney. And then there was Jack. Jack was seven, with a cowlick and big ears, and he was lonely, honey. He'd wander into the kitchen at night when Helen was cleaning up, and sit on the counter, and talk to her. About dinosaurs, and his teacher, and how his daddy never had time to play. And Helen would give him a cookie she'd hidden in her apron.""",
   "little boy sitting on kitchen counter at night", "A LONELY LITTLE BOY")
sc("""On the fourth night, Jack looked at her for a long time while she washed the dishes. And then he said, Nell, why do you hum like that? Helen always hummed Silent Night when she washed dishes. She'd done it her whole life. And Jack said, my grandma hums like that. On the phone, at my birthday. Daddy lets me call her sometimes when Mom's not home. You sound just like her.""",
   "grandmother washing dishes humming", "YOU SOUND JUST LIKE HER")
sc("""And then that little boy looked at her hands in the dishwater, and he said, very quietly, you have a ring mark on your finger. Grandma has a ring with a green stone. Are you my grandma? Helen said she couldn't breathe. She took off her drugstore glasses. And Jack threw his arms around her neck and said, I knew it. I knew it. I won't tell. I promise I won't tell.""",
   "little boy hugging grandmother in kitchen", "I KNEW IT")

ch("The secret")
sc("""For the next week, Jack and his grandmother had a secret, honey. Every night, he'd sneak down to the kitchen, and she'd tell him stories about when his daddy was little. How Brian was afraid of the dark until he was ten. How he cried at the end of every Lassie episode. How he'd saved up his allowance for a whole year to buy his mother a music box for Christmas. A music box that played Silent Night.""",
   "vintage music box on nightstand", "THE MUSIC BOX")
sc("""And Jack told her things too. That his mother said Grandma was mean, and sick, and wanted to take Daddy's money. That Daddy cried once, in his study, holding an old music box, and Jack didn't know why. And Helen sat at that kitchen island in her apron at midnight, holding her grandson's hand, and realized her son wasn't happy either. Not one person in that big house was happy.""",
   "man alone in study holding music box", "DADDY CRIED")

sc("""Two nights before Christmas, Helen almost quit, honey. Courtney found a cookie crumb on Jack's pajamas and screamed at Nell in front of everybody, for feeding the children sugar without permission. She called her a useless old woman and threatened to call the agency. Helen stood there in her apron and took it. And when Courtney left the room, Emma, the twelve-year-old, quietly brought her a glass of water and said, I'm sorry my mom yelled.""",
   "girl bringing glass of water to maid", "I'M SORRY MY MOM YELLED")
sc("""That night, Helen called me and said, Ruth, I'm done. I've seen enough. I'll leave in the morning, and I'll never tell Brian. It would only hurt him. And I said, Helen, what about Jack? What about Christmas morning? And there was a long silence on the phone. And then she said, you're right. That little boy is keeping a secret for me. I can't leave him holding it alone.""",
   "elderly woman on phone at night thinking", "I CAN'T LEAVE HIM")
ch("Christmas morning")
sc("""On Christmas morning, Courtney had arranged everything for a photographer from a magazine. The tree was twenty feet tall. Everybody was in matching red sweaters. And Helen was in the corner in her apron, holding a tray of coffee. And then Jack stood up in the middle of all those presents and said, wait, I have a present for Daddy. And he ran to the kitchen and came back pulling Helen by the hand.""",
   "boy pulling maid by hand into living room", "A PRESENT FOR DADDY")
sc("""He stood her in front of his father, and he pulled off her gray wig, and he said, Daddy, it's Grandma. She's been here the whole time. You walked past her every day. The photographer stopped clicking. Courtney dropped her coffee cup. And Brian stood up slowly, honey, and stared at his mother's face, with her auburn hair falling out of the bobby pins, and his face fell apart.""",
   "man staring shocked at his mother", "IT'S GRANDMA")
sc("""He said, Mom? Mom, you were the maid? And Helen said, I wanted to see you, Brian. And I wanted to know if you were happy. You're not. And her son, forty-eight years old, a Harvard man in a matching red sweater, put his face in his mother's apron and cried like a little boy. In front of the photographer, and Courtney's parents, and everybody.""",
   "grown son crying in mother's arms", "YOU'RE NOT HAPPY")

ch("What happened after")
sc("""Courtney screamed that it was a trick, and that Helen was crazy, just like she'd always said. But Jack said, Mommy, you told everybody Grandma was mean. She's not. She's the nicest person in this whole house. And twelve-year-old Emma, who'd been quiet the whole time, said, it's true, Mom. You told us she was sick. You lied. Nobody in that room could argue with two children.""",
   "children confronting mother by Christmas tree", "YOU LIED")
sc("""Brian and Courtney separated that spring, sweetheart. I won't say more about that, because it's their business. But Brian moved back to Mansfield with the children. He took a job running his father's old valve company. And every Thursday now, he brings Helen to my kitchen for coffee, and Jack eats all my cookies, and I let him have three.""",
   "father and children at grandmother's kitchen", "EVERY THURSDAY")

ch("Grandma's lesson")
sc("""Helen is eighty-two now, honey, same as me. Jack is seventeen. And every Christmas Eve, he puts on that gray wig and an apron and serves everybody coffee, and the whole family laughs until they cry. And on Helen's nightstand is a music box that plays Silent Night. Brian kept it for forty years. He gave it back to her that Christmas.""",
   "music box on nightstand Christmas night", "IT PLAYS SILENT NIGHT")
sc("""Here's what I want you to remember, sweetheart. Children always know the truth. Listen to the quiet ones. Tell me in the comments, honey, would you have done what Helen did? There are more of my family's stories in the playlist right there on your screen, and I'll see you in the next one. Good night. Merry Christmas.""",
   "mansion window Christmas night snow", "WOULD YOU HAVE DONE IT?")

